
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">&#8220;Our central ambition is philosophy on demand: philosophical judgment of exceptional depth and quality, available quickly enough to inform consequential decisions.&#8221;</p>



<span id="more-27387"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">That&#8217;s <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/graham-clay-b78025a1" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Graham Clay</a>, writing about Pensatura, an AI philosophy system he and <a href="https://www.calebontiveros.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Caleb Ontiveros</a> are developing. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Pensatura &#8220;coordinates AI tasks that investigate sources, develop arguments, examine objections and construct an overall answer [to a philosophical question], retaining the resulting work so later inquiries can use, challenge and extend it. Each argument in the record states its premises, taken jointly, and an explicit claim about how they support its conclusion. That support claim can itself have grounds and face objections. Interpretations preserve their sources and rival readings; improvements are distinguished from what an author actually argued. The intended result is an expanding body of reasoning whose conclusions remain connected to the grounds on which they depend.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">They report that they&#8217;ve built a &#8220;working prototype&#8221; of Pensatura that includes the foregoing, makes use of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lean_(proof_assistant)" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Lean</a> to check formalized arguments, and includes &#8220;machinery for probabilistic assessment.&#8221; At the moment, its scope is philosophical questions about AI research, but they plan to expand beyond that: &#8220;the engine&#8217;s ambition is general.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Clay and Ontiveros ultimately see Pensatura as a way of making philosophical knowledge more readily available to decision-makers whose work could be improved by it. It might also make possible the kind of computer-aided philosophy I speculated about <a href="https://dailynous.com/2021/03/24/hey-sophi-or-how-much-philosophy-will-computers-do/">here</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I will note that I am ambivalent about this kind of project. I have doubts about the answer-based conception of philosophy at its core, concerns about the drive it embodies towards what we might call &#8220;philosophical efficiency,&#8221; and worries about what such technology might one day replace. Nonetheless, it is an interesting experiment, and if it comes close to working, it will be a fascinating, impressive, and philosophically important piece of technology.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the following guest post, Clay provides more details on the project and its motivation, and solicits help from other philosophers. (The post was written with assistance from an AI.)</p>



